talk(284,'1972-11-20','I. M. Gelfand','P000474-P000475','Arnold’s simple singularities, versal deformations and vanishing cycles',r'''The intervention occupies P000474--P000475, after Kostyuchenko and before Buchstaber\textquotesingle s continuation. Gelfand explains Arnold\textquotesingle s ADE classification, versality through the Jacobian algebra, polynomial discriminants and braid groups, then relates Milnor fibres and intersection forms to Dynkin diagrams. The codimension-six sentence uses Arnold\textquotesingle s convention for critical-point strata, not the dimension of a miniversal base. The closing local-index/asymptotic programme and the planned report on Maslov are preserved as a programme, rather than expanded into an absent theorem.''')
item(284,'P000474','definition','Function germs and the equivalence of hypersurfaces',r'The notes start with \(f:(\mathbb R^n,0)\to(\mathbb R,0)\), \(f(0)=df(0)=0\), then specify that the actual setting is complex. Hypersurfaces are equivalent under changes of variables.',r'The subsequent formulas concern holomorphic function germs \(f\in\mathcal O_{\mathbb C^n,0}\) with an isolated critical point, under right equivalence \(g=f\circ\phi\), where \(\phi\) is a local biholomorphism fixing the origin. Equivalence of reduced zero hypersurfaces is the weaker contact relation \(g=u(f\circ\phi)\) with \(u\) a unit; it should not be substituted into the Jacobian-algebra versality formula without adjustment.',r'The function-germ convention is explicit in \citet[section 2]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],reconstruction='The distinction between right and contact equivalence is supplied because the notebook alternates the words function and hypersurface.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000474','theorem','Simple germs and the ADE list',r'A simple hypersurface has only finitely many nearby jet orbits, uniformly in sufficiently high jet order. The \(A,D,E\) diagrams are drawn.',r'A finitely determined holomorphic germ is right-simple if a sufficiently small neighborhood of a sufficiently high jet meets only finitely many right-equivalence classes. Up to adding nondegenerate quadratic forms, the simple isolated critical-point germs are \(A_k:x^{k+1}\) for \(k\ge1\), \(D_k:x^2y+y^{k-1}\) for \(k\ge4\), and \(E_6:x^3+y^4\), \(E_7:x^3+xy^3\), \(E_8:x^3+y^5\).',r'Exact prior source: \citet[Theorem 2.10 and section 7]{arnold1972normal}, received 21 June 1972, published in the fourth issue of 1972.',[ARN],reconstruction='The whole list makes the diagrams explicit; the manuscript writes only the A-series normal form at this point.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000474','claim','The first nonsimple stratum has codimension six',r'\ru{Непростые коразмерности 6}; most orbits are called simple.',r'In the space of sufficiently high jets with a critical point at the origin and critical value zero, nonsimple germs first occur in codimension six when at least three essential variables are allowed. In two variables the first such locus has codimension seven. This does not say that every nonsimple germ has codimension six, nor that every simple germ has codimension below six: \(E_7\) and \(E_8\) are simple examples of higher codimension.',r'\citet[Introduction and Theorem 7.1]{arnold1972normal} states precisely these thresholds. Thus six is not a transcription error.',[ARN],reconstruction='The ambient stratum and the phrase first occur replace an otherwise ambiguous shorthand. Its codimension convention differs from the Milnor number.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(284,'P000474','definition','Versal deformation and its pullback property',r'Every deformation \(G(x,\mu)\) is written \(F(X(x,\mu),\Psi(\mu))\), with \(X(y,0)=y\) and \(\Psi(0)=0\).',r'A holomorphic unfolding \(F:(\mathbb C^n\times\mathbb C^r,0)\to\mathbb C\), \(F(x,0)=f(x)\), is right-versal if every holomorphic unfolding \(G(y,\mu)\) of \(f\) is induced by a holomorphic parameter map \(\Psi\) fixing zero and a parameter-dependent local biholomorphism \(X\) with \(X(y,0)=y\). The equality is one of function germs in both variables; the moving critical point need not remain the origin for nonzero parameters.',r'Exact definition: \citet[Definition 8.1]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000474','method','A transverse slice produces a versal family',r'Take the orbit and a submanifold transverse to it; a sketch illustrates the construction.',r'For an isolated critical point, choose a sufficiently high jet order so that finite determinacy applies. A finite-dimensional family whose parameter tangent directions surject onto the quotient of function germs by the Jacobian ideal is infinitesimally right-versal and hence right-versal. An arbitrary transverse picture in an unspecified infinite-dimensional function space is not itself a proof.',r'The operative implication is \citet[Theorem 8.3]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],reconstruction='The infinitesimal criterion and finite-determinacy setting supply the meaning of the geometric sketch.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact')
item(284,'P000474','theorem','The Jacobian algebra and infinitesimal versality',r'Every \(a(x)\) must be \(\sum_i(\partial_i f)h_i+\sum_jc_j\varphi_j\); the quotient by the Jacobian ideal is finite-dimensional, of dimension \(k\).',r'For a holomorphic germ with an isolated critical point, \(Q_f=\mathcal O_{\mathbb C^n,0}/(\partial_1f,\ldots,\partial_nf)\) has finite dimension \(\mu\). An unfolding is infinitesimally right-versal precisely when the classes of \(\partial_{\lambda_j}F(x,0)\) span \(Q_f\) as a complex vector space. If these classes form a basis, the family is miniversal with \(\mu\) parameters. Finite dimensionality fails in general for nonisolated critical loci.',r'Exact source: \citet[Definition 8.2, Theorem 8.3 and Corollary 8.4]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],reconstruction='The notebook word generate means vector-space spanning in the quotient, not generation of the full local ring. The isolated-critical-point assumption is essential.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000475','construction','The Ak Jacobian algebra and miniversal unfolding',r'For \(f=z_1^{k+1}+z_2^2+\cdots+z_n^2\), the derivatives are written without their numerical factors; the listed parameters multiply \(1,z_1,\ldots,z_1^{k-1}\).',r'Over \(\mathbb C\), \(J_f=((k+1)z_1^k,2z_2,\ldots,2z_n)=(z_1^k,z_2,\ldots,z_n)\). Hence \(Q_f\simeq\mathbb C[z_1]/(z_1^k)\), and \(F=f+\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\lambda_jz_1^j\) is a miniversal right-unfolding. The numerical derivative factors may be omitted from ideal generators, but not from equalities of derivatives.',r'This is \citet[Corollary 8.4, A-series]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],reconstruction='The ambiguous early subscripts are normalized to 1 using the Jacobian quotient and the later displayed family; the scan itself retains i-like marks.',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(284,'P000475','definition','The discriminant and its generic Morse points',r'The bifurcation diagram consists of parameters for which singular points remain; on an open set of the diagram the singularities are called Morse.',r'For a sufficiently small representative of a miniversal unfolding of an isolated critical point, its discriminant is \(\Delta=\{\lambda:\exists x,\ F(x,\lambda)=0,\ d_xF(x,\lambda)=0\}\). A generic smooth point of \(\Delta\) corresponds to one nondegenerate critical point at critical value zero. Thus the generic singular zero fibre has type \(A_1\); off \(\Delta\) the zero fibre is smooth.',r'The definition and ADE discriminants appear in \citet[section 9, opening paragraph]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],reconstruction='Generic smooth stratum replaces the unspecified open set. The word simple here means Morse, which is narrower than the ADE notion of a simple germ.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000475','construction','The polynomial discriminant and the swallowtail name',r'For \(z_1^{k+1}+z_2^2\), singularity means \(P(z_1)=P^{\prime}(z_1)=0\); the diagram is called a swallowtail.',r'For \(P(t)=t^{k+1}+\lambda_{k-1}t^{k-1}+\cdots+\lambda_0\), the zero fibre \(P(z_1)+z_2^2=0\) is singular exactly when \(P\) has a multiple root, so \(\Delta\) is its discriminant hypersurface. The ordinary swallowtail is the \(A_3\) discriminant; general \(A_k\) gives the corresponding higher discriminant, also called a generalized swallowtail in Arnold\textquotesingle s terminology.',r'The qualification generalized and the special \(A_3\) case are explicit in \citet[section 9]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],reconstruction='The general label is not treated as a false formula; its special conventional meaning is clarified.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000475','theorem','The discriminant complement has the corresponding Artin group',r'The fundamental group of the complement of the bifurcation diagram is the braid group of a Coxeter group.',r'For a simple ADE germ, the complement of its miniversal discriminant is identified with the regular orbit space \(\mathfrak h_{\mathbb C}^{\mathrm{reg}}/W\). Its fundamental group is the Artin group of that ADE Coxeter system: adjacent generators satisfy the braid relation of length three and nonadjacent generators commute. For \(A_k\) this is the ordinary braid group \(B_{k+1}\), not the finite Weyl group itself.',r'\citet[Proposition 9.3]{arnold1972normal} gives the singularity identification; \citet[theorem, original pp.~57--58; Russian translation pp.~147--148]{brieskorn1971fundamental} gives the presentation. The original publication is1971; the opened full text is its1975 Russian translation.',[ARN,BRI],reconstruction='The ADE restriction is necessary. The notebook does not assert this identification for all isolated singularities.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000475','theorem','The Milnor fibre is a bouquet of middle-dimensional spheres',r'A small nonsingular deformed fibre has homology only in degree \(n-1\) and is a bouquet of \(\mu\) spheres.',r'Let \(f:(\mathbb C^n,0)\to(\mathbb C,0)\), \(n\ge2\), be holomorphic with an isolated critical point. For Milnor radii \(0<|\delta|\ll\varepsilon\), \(F_\delta=f^{-1}(\delta)\cap B_\varepsilon\) has the homotopy type of \(\bigvee_\mu S^{n-1}\), with \(\mu=\dim_{\mathbb C}Q_f\). Thus reduced integral homology vanishes outside degree \(n-1\), where it is \(\mathbb Z^\mu\); ordinary \(H_0\) is also nonzero.',r'\citet[Theorems 6.5 and 7.2, Appendix B, Problem 3]{milnor1968singular} proves the general assertion; the ADE case is \citet[Proposition 9.4]{arnold1972normal}.',[MIL,ARN],reconstruction='The word reduced repairs the homology sentence. Smallness and transversality are required; an arbitrary regular fibre in an arbitrary ball need not have this type.',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(284,'P000475','construction','Intersection form and the sign of vanishing cycles',r'For \(n-1\equiv2\pmod4\), vanishing cycles satisfy \(e_i^2=-2\); negative definite, semidefinite and indefinite possibilities are listed.',r'On \(H_{n-1}(F_\delta;\mathbb Z)\), the middle intersection pairing is symmetric when \(n-1\) is even and skew-symmetric when it is odd. For an oriented vanishing sphere in complex dimension \(n-1\equiv2\pmod4\), the self-intersection is \(-2\). It is \(+2\) when \(n-1\equiv0\pmod4\). Definite, degenerate and indefinite forms all occur among isolated hypersurface singularities; ADE gives the negative Cartan lattice in the stated congruence.',r'The conventions and ADE cycles are explicit in \citet[Proposition 9.4]{arnold1972normal}; signatures beyond ADE are discussed in \citet[section 1]{arnold1973unimodal}.',[ARN,ARN73],reconstruction='The notebook uses cohomology notation for classes subsequently intersected; homology vanishing cycles are used here. The word always is restricted to the displayed congruence.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(284,'P000475','construction','Monodromy on the vanishing-cycle lattice',r'Monodromy is described only as a representation of the fundamental group, followed by an unfinished target.',r'The smooth family over a discriminant complement defines \(\rho:\pi_1(B\setminus\Delta,\lambda_0)\to\operatorname{Aut}(H_{n-1}(F_{\lambda_0};\mathbb Z))\), preserving the intersection pairing. For ADE and odd \(n\), Picard--Lefschetz transformations act as the corresponding root reflections, so this representation factors through the Weyl group.',r'\citet[Proposition 9.5]{arnold1972normal} states this precise ADE realization.',[ARN],reconstruction='The target and the ADE factorization are supplied from the explicitly identified source; the notebook itself breaks off after the word representation.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(284,'P000475','remark','A programme linking local asymptotics and global topology',r'Gelfand asks how to remove derivatives from Singer\textquotesingle s calculations and study global topology while sitting at one point; explicit asymptotics for second-order equations and a future Shnirelman report on Maslov are proposed.',r'This is a programme rather than a defined conjecture: no operator class, coefficient formula, equivalence relation or target estimate is stated. The bounded identifiable themes are local proofs of index formulas and semiclassical/asymptotic methods; the named future talk is not itself evidence of a theorem.',r'No exact publication can be matched to the undefined request. The later APS intervention in record296 is a thematic crosslink, not proof that this programme was completed in precisely the intended form.',[],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='the proposed calculation remains unidentified at the necessary level of specificity, so no research problem or C/D item is manufactured.')
correction('P000474','simple-hypersurface equivalence and versal formula',r'Equivalence of hypersurfaces under coordinate changes; Jacobian quotient criterion',r'Distinguish right equivalence of functions from contact equivalence of defining equations.',r'The later deformation equality is for functions and the quotient uses only partial derivatives, identifying right-versality. Equivalence of zero sets alone also allows multiplication by a unit.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000474','nonsimple codimension',r'Nonsimple of codimension6',r'First nonsimple locus has codimension6 for n≥3 under Arnold\textquotesingle s critical-point-stratum convention; codimension7 for n=2.',r'The scan genuinely says6. Arnold1972 Introduction and Theorem7.1 confirm it. Do not replace6 by a Milnor-number threshold.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000475','derivatives in Ak example',r'∂f/∂z1=z1^k; ∂f/∂z2=z2',r'∂f/∂z1=(k+1)z1^k; ∂f/∂zj=2zj for j≥2.',r'The omitted factors are visible in the manuscript; the generated ideal is unchanged over C, but the displayed derivative equalities are false.')
correction('P000475','local-algebra basis',r'1,z_i,z_i²,...,z_1^{k−1}',r'1,z_1,z_1²,...,z_1^{k−1}.',r'The first subscripts are i-like in the scan. The Jacobian quotient kills every z_j with j≥2, and the next unfolding uses z1 throughout.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000475','homology of the smooth fibre',r'Homology nonzero only in middle degree n−1',r'Reduced homology nonzero only in middle degree; ordinary H0 also occurs.',r'The scan has no reduced qualifier. A nonempty connected Milnor fibre has H0=Z; Milnor1968 Theorems6.5 and7.2 give the exact result.')
