talk(294,'1973-03-19','V. I. Arnold','P000491-P000492','Modality and the intersection forms of simple and unimodal singularities',r'''Arnold\textquotesingle s actual passage begins on P000491 and ends above Mishchenko\textquotesingle s name on P000492. It recalls finite determinacy and the ADE classification, then links Milnor lattices to definiteness and turns to modality one. The unimodal announcement is identified exactly: Arnold\textquotesingle s paper was received 12 March 1973, one week before this seminar, and published later in 1973. The notebook compresses several different signature statements into false implications; these are checked against Arnold\textquotesingle s announcement, Tyurina\textquotesingle s 1968 paper and Gabrielov\textquotesingle s intersection-matrix calculation.''')
item(294,'P000491','definition','Finite determinacy and the action on jets',r'For \(f:(\mathbb C^n,0)\to(\mathbb C,0)\), the group of \(k\)-jets of diffeomorphisms acts on function jets; a sufficient jet means that later terms do not matter.',r'A holomorphic germ \(f\) is right \(k\)-determined if every holomorphic germ \(g\) with \(j^kg=j^kf\) satisfies \(g=f\circ\phi\) for a local biholomorphism \(\phi\) fixing zero. An isolated critical point gives finite determinacy for some \(k\); no particular universal jet order is specified here.',r'This is the finite-jet framework of \citet[sections 2--3]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Isolatedness, holomorphic coordinate changes and the existence rather than a prescribed value of k are made explicit.')
item(294,'P000491-P000492','definition','Modality counts families of nearby orbits',r'\(m\) is the number of moduli; zero modality means only finitely many possibilities after perturbing coefficients.',r'For a sufficiently high jet of a finitely determined germ, its modality is the least \(m\) such that a neighborhood is covered by finitely many families of right-equivalence orbits depending on at most \(m\) parameters. It is a local orbit-family dimension, not the dimension of the miniversal base \(\mu\), nor simply the number of coefficients in a chosen polynomial.',r'Exact definition: \citet[section 1]{arnold1973unimodal}, submitted 12 March 1973; the zero-modality case is already defined in \citet[section 2]{arnold1972normal}.',[ARN73,ARN],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(294,'P000491','theorem','Zero modality is exactly ADE',r'The list is \(A_k:x^{k+1}\), \(D_k:x^2y+y^{k-1}\), \(E_6:x^3+y^4\), \(E_7:x^3+xy^3\), \(E_8:x^3+y^5\).',r'The complete list of holomorphic isolated right-simple germs up to stable equivalence is the ADE list of 1972-284.2, with \(k\ge1\) for \(A_k\) and \(k\ge4\) for \(D_k\). Conversely each listed type has modality zero.',r'Published before this talk in \citet[Theorem 2.10, Corollary 8.6]{arnold1972normal}; the1973 announcement recalls it as the zero-modal case.',[ARN],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(294,'P000491','definition','Stable equivalence and the inappropriate positivity condition',r'Stable equivalence means adding nondegenerate quadratic forms; the next sentence says the quadratic form is positive definite.',r'Two complex holomorphic germs are stably right-equivalent if, after adding nondegenerate complex quadratic forms in auxiliary variables, they become right-equivalent. Every nondegenerate complex quadratic form is equivalent to a sum of squares; positivity is not an intrinsic notion over \(\mathbb C\). For real germs the signature of the quadratic summand is relevant and need not be positive.',r'\citet[section 1]{arnold1973unimodal} uses nondegenerate forms. The real signatures in \citet[Introduction, formula (1.1)]{arnold1972normal} also rule out a blanket positivity requirement.',[ARN73,ARN],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The handwritten positivity sentence is faithfully reproduced; its correct replacement depends on the coefficient field.',development='annotated')
item(294,'P000492','remark','The unresolved sentence about analytic coordinate changes',r'\ru{Аналит. ф-я с точн. до анал. зам. перем. однозн. опр. зам. перем.}',r'The surviving line does not specify what determines what. If read as uniqueness of the analytic normalizing coordinate change, it is false: even a nondegenerate quadratic form has nontrivial linear symmetries. Finite determinacy concerns existence of an equivalence, not uniqueness of a coordinate map.',r'The surrounding finite-determinacy and modality definitions identify the subject but not the missing assertion. No exact publication is assigned to this line.',[],cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',reconstruction='The tautological diplomatic reading is retained; replacing its final words by Taylor polynomial or another guessed object would go beyond the scan.',confidence='low',status='the intended assertion remains unreadable at the level needed for a mathematical identification.')
item(294,'P000492','construction','The local smooth level manifold',r'\(f^{-1}(\varepsilon)\cap B_\delta\) is a manifold with boundary of even real dimension.',r'For a holomorphic germ with isolated critical point and radii \(0<|\varepsilon|\ll\delta\), transverse intersection with the closed ball gives a compact smooth real \(2n-2\)-manifold with boundary, whose interior is a complex \((n-1)\)-manifold. Its diffeomorphism type is independent of admissible sufficiently small choices.',r'The admissible radius/value construction is explicit in \citet[section 1.1]{tyurina1968topological}; see also \citet[sections 4--6]{milnor1968singular}.',[TYU,MIL],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The required order of smallness and boundary transversality are supplied.')
item(294,'P000492','theorem','Tyurina’s definiteness criterion',r'The middle-dimensional integral intersection form is negative definite exactly in the listed ADE cases when \(n\equiv3\pmod4\); otherwise add variables. The attribution reads \ru{теор. Тюрина}.',r'After stabilization to \(n\equiv3\pmod4\), the Milnor intersection form of an isolated complex hypersurface critical point is negative definite precisely for the simple ADE types. In complex surface dimension two this is the equivalence between negative-definite smoothing intersection form and a rational double point. Other congruences require the appropriate suspension and sign convention.',r'The original surface result is \citet[section 3, Lemma 3, together with Theorem 2]{tyurina1968topological}, received 13 May 1967, published1968. \citet[section 4, Remarks 3--4]{arnold1973unimodal} explicitly attributes the elliptic/ADE classification to G. N. Tyurina and states it in stable form.',[TYU,ARN73],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The attribution is to Galina N. Tyurina. Russian Тюрина here is the genitive form and should not be normalized as the surname of a different male author.')
item(294,'P000492','theorem','Milnor number, bouquet and local algebra',r'The level manifold is a bouquet of \(\mu\) copies of \(S^{n-1}\), and \(\mu\) is the dimension of the local ring.',r'The precise statement is 1972-284.11: the Milnor number is \(\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathcal O/(\partial f)\), not the dimension of the whole local ring \(\mathcal O\), which is infinite. This finite number is the rank of reduced middle homology and the number of spheres in the bouquet.',r'\citet[Theorems 6.5 and 7.2, Appendix B, Problem 3]{milnor1968singular}; the terminology local ring of the gradient map is used by \citet[section 1]{arnold1973unimodal}.',[MIL,ARN73],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Local ring is read as the finite local algebra of the gradient mapping, consistently with record284.')
item(294,'P000492','construction','The E6 intersection diagram',r'An \(E_6\) graph is labelled \(-2\) at a vertex and \(1\) at an edge; the quadratic form is said to be given by the Dynkin diagram.',r'In a suitable distinguished basis of the Milnor lattice of \(E_6\), stabilized to complex fibre dimension \(2\bmod4\), the Gram matrix has diagonal \(-2\), entry \(1\) for adjacent vertices, and entry \(0\) otherwise. It is the negative \(E_6\) Cartan matrix. The diagram depends on a basis; it is not an unlabelled invariant for arbitrary singularities.',r'\citet[Proposition 9.4]{arnold1972normal} gives the ADE rule. An explicit basis transformation for \(E_6\) is \citet[section 6, Example 2]{gabrielov1973intersection}, received29January1973 and published later that year.',[ARN,GAB],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(294,'P000492','theorem','The unimodal classification announcement',r'For \(m=1\) there is also a long list; the first displayed example is \(x_1^3+x_2^3+x_3^3\).',r'Up to stable right equivalence, the isolated holomorphic germs of modality one consist of the three-index \(T_{pqr}\) series, including its three parabolic cases, and fourteen exceptional one-parameter families. The latter are named \(E_{12},E_{13},E_{14},Z_{11},Z_{12},Z_{13},Q_{10},Q_{11},Q_{12},W_{12},W_{13},S_{11},S_{12},U_{12}\). The notebook does not list these fourteen normal forms.',r'Exact announcement: \citet[sections 2--3]{arnold1973unimodal}, received12March1973, published1973. \citet[Introduction, section 1.4]{arnold1974normal} gives the organized T-series/fourteen-family formulation; received28September1973, published1974.',[ARN73,ARN74],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The family names and organization are reconstructed from the exact publication; the unrecorded normal-form table is not passed off as notebook content.',status='the stated unimodal classification is established. Its published scope is isolated holomorphic right-equivalence classes up to stabilization.')
item(294,'P000492','construction','The cubic example is simple elliptic with a two-dimensional radical',r'The first modality-one example is \(x_1^3+x_2^3+x_3^3\).',r'The cubic \(f=x^3+y^3+z^3\) has \(\mu=8\), modality one and intersection-form inertia \((\mu_+,\mu_0,\mu_-)=(0,2,6)\). It belongs to the family \(x^3+y^3+z^3+a xyz\), with \(a^3\ne-27\) for an isolated critical point. Its Milnor lattice splits as a negative \(E_6\) lattice plus a rank-two zero form.',r'\citet[section 6, Example 4 and Proposition 5]{gabrielov1973intersection} gives the lattice and corank two. The paper records Milnor\textquotesingle s letter to Arnold of31October1972 about the parabolic monodromy groups; Gabrielov\textquotesingle s manuscript was received29January1973.',[GAB,ARN73],cls='A',reconstruction=r'The modulus and its excluded discriminant value are supplied. The notebook example alone already shows why a blanket nullity-one assertion is unsafe; the exact corank is read in the original calculation.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(294,'P000492','claim','The signature implications need two separate corrections',r'With \(\mu=\mu_++\mu_0+\mu_-\), the notebook appears to assert \(\mu_+\le1\Rightarrow m=1\), and explicitly writes \(\mu_+\le1\Rightarrow\mu_0=1\).',r'Neither displayed implication is true as an unrestricted assertion. ADE forms have \(\mu_+=0\), \(\mu_0=0\), modality zero. The cubic in1972-294.11 has \(\mu_+=0\), \(\mu_0=2\), modality one. The matching valid result in Arnold\textquotesingle s announcement is that the cases with \(\mu_+=1\) are the nonparabolic T-series; they have \(\mu_0=1\). This does not assert that every unimodal singularity has \(\mu_+\le1\).',r'\citet[section 3, Corollary Zh]{arnold1973unimodal} gives the precise positive-index-one statement; \citet[Proposition 5]{gabrielov1973intersection} supplies the decisive cubic counterexample.',[ARN73,GAB],cls='A',literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The first inequality is marked doubtful in the diplomatic transcription, so its exact sign remains an ambiguity. The second ≤1 is clear in the scan and is a faithful mathematical error. No silent replacement of both inequalities by equalities is made.',development='annotated',status='the literal implications are false; the correctly restricted positive-index-one classification is established.')
item(294,'P000492','construction','The hyperbolic T-series and its missing restrictions',r'Under \(\mu_+=1\), \(axyz+x^k+y^\ell+z^m\) is called the three-index series \(T_{k\ell m}\).',r'For integers \(p,q,r\ge2\) with \(1/p+1/q+1/r<1\) and \(a\ne0\), the germ \(x^p+y^q+z^r+a xyz\) is of hyperbolic type \(T_{pqr}\), with \(\mu=p+q+r-1\), modality one and \((\mu_+,\mu_0)=(1,1)\) after the stated stabilization. Equality \(1/p+1/q+1/r=1\) gives the parabolic types \((3,3,3),(2,4,4),(2,3,6)\), with parameter values restricted to keep the critical point isolated, and with a different signature.',r'The T-series and inertia-one characterization are \citet[section 3]{arnold1973unimodal}; the reciprocal-sum split is explicit in \citet[section 1.4]{arnold1974normal}.',[ARN73,ARN74],cls='A',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Exponent restrictions, the nonzero coefficient and the hyperbolic/parabolic distinction are supplied. The three integers label a series; they are not three continuous moduli.')
correction('P000491','quadratic stabilization',r'The quadratic form is positive definite',r'Over C use a nondegenerate quadratic form, equivalent to a sum of squares; over R retain its signature.',r'The scan plainly says positive definite while the germ is explicitly complex. Arnold1973 definition uses nondegenerate quadratic forms.')
correction('P000492','first line about analytic changes',r'An analytic function up to analytic variable change uniquely determines a variable change',r'Unresolved compressed sentence; do not impose uniqueness of a normalizing map.',r'The scan repeats abbreviated зам. перем. at both ends and does not supply the missing object. Quadratic-form symmetries refute uniqueness if that is the intended literal meaning.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000492','Tyurina attribution',r'Tyurin\textquotesingle s theorem',r'Tyurina\textquotesingle s theorem (G. N. Tyurina).',r'The Russian reads теор. Тюрина, which is consistent with the genitive of Тюрина. Arnold1973 section4 Remark3 explicitly attributes the theorem to G.N.Tyurina and cites her1968 paper.',applies='en',kind='translation')
correction('P000492','signature and modality',r'μ+≤1 implies m=1',r'The ≤ sign is doubtful; the matching published nonparabolic theorem assumes μ+=1. As a literal ≤1 assertion it fails for ADE.',r'Crop inspection confirms the conclusion m=1 but the first inequality is retraced. Arnold1973 section3 CorollaryZh uses μ+=1; ADE provides the check.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000492','nullity claim',r'μ+≤1 implies μ0=1 (always degenerate)',r'The positive-index-one T cases have μ0=1; simple elliptic cases have μ+=0 and μ0=2, while ADE has μ0=0.',r'The second ≤ is clear in the scan. Gabrielov1973 Proposition5 gives corank2 for the cubic written immediately above; Arnold1973 gives the μ+=1 theorem.')
