talk(295,'1973-03-19','A. S. Mishchenko','P000492-P000494','Sullivan’s localization, completion and proof of the Adams conjecture',r'''The report starts under Mishchenko\textquotesingle s name in the lower part of P000492 and ends with the comparison of analytic and étale homotopy on P000494. It explains the Adams conjecture through completed Grassmannians, spherical fibrations and Galois symmetry. Its source is identifiable as Sullivan\textquotesingle s June1970 MIT notes, whose title is almost quoted; the expanded Annals paper appeared in1974. Thus the material was already circulating, and Quillen\textquotesingle s independent proof had appeared in1971. The report continues the Adams-operation and étale-homotopy discussions1971-259.2 and1971-262.1--.3. The broad catalogue range also contains Arnold, APS and the p-adic report, which are not assigned to Mishchenko.''')
item(295,'P000492','definition','The finite reduced J-group',r'\(K(X)/J\text{-equivalence}=J(X)\) is called a finite group.',r'For a connected finite CW-complex \(X\), quotient the reduced real K-group \(\widetilde{KO}(X)\) by the subgroup of stably fibre-homotopy-trivial virtual bundles. The resulting reduced J-group is finite. The corresponding complex version uses underlying real sphere bundles. If unreduced K-theory and actual ranks are retained, the quotient also has a \(\mathbb Z\) rank summand and is not finite.',r'\citet[section1, p.~181]{adams1963groups} explicitly separates \(J(X)=\mathbb Z\oplus\widetilde J(X)\) and quotes Atiyah\textquotesingle s earlier finiteness theorem; received29May1963 and published1963.',[AD1],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Finite CW type, reduction and stable equivalence are necessary to make the displayed finiteness assertion correct.',development='annotated')
item(295,'P000492','definition','Adams operations determined on line bundles',r'\(\psi^k(\xi)=\xi^k\) for \(\dim\xi=1\), together with additivity, determines the operations.',r'For complex topological K-theory on finite CW-complexes, \(\psi^k\) is the natural ring endomorphism satisfying \(\psi^k[L]=[L^{\otimes k}]\) on line bundles. Naturality, the splitting principle and additivity determine it on all classes. It preserves virtual rank and obeys \(\psi^k\psi^\ell=\psi^{k\ell}\). Its value on a higher-rank bundle is generally a virtual bundle, not a specified bundle of the same actual rank.',r'\citet[pp.~162--163]{sullivan1970geometric} states the operations and Newton-polynomial construction; \citet[p.~7]{sullivan1974genetics} gives the line-bundle characterization. Both explain the pre-existing operations.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Naturality and the splitting principle are supplied; additivity on a single fixed K-group without these conditions would not give uniqueness.')
item(295,'P000492','definition','Fibrewise degree one and J-equivalence',r'J-equivalence is fibrewise homotopy equivalence; a proper bundle map is required to have degree1 on every fibre.',r'Two real vector bundles of equal rank are stably J-equivalent when their unit-sphere bundles become fibre-homotopy equivalent after adding trivial bundles. A continuous proper fibre-preserving map, of degree \(+1\) on the one-point compactification of each oriented vector-space fibre, supplies such a stable equivalence: those compactifications are the sphere bundles after one additional trivial summand. A linear isomorphism is not required.',r'\citet[section1]{adams1963groups} defines the quotient through sphere bundles. The fibre-degree construction and its localization variant are used in \citet[section2, Lemma2.3]{quillen1971adams}.',[AD1,QUI],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Stabilization, equal dimensions and the relation between unit spheres and fibrewise one-point compactification distinguish the two versions of the notebook definition.')
item(295,'P000492','theorem','Recovering the J-quotient from K-theory and all Adams operations',r'The group \(J(X)\) is determined by \(K(X)\) as a module over the operations.',r'For finite CW-complexes, Adams\textquotesingle s algebraic lower and conjectural upper J-quotients of real K-theory agree; the Adams conjecture identifies this common quotient with the topological J-quotient. The algebraic construction uses the subgroup generated by suitable \(k^{e(k)}(\psi^k-1)y\) as \(k\) varies, with sufficiently large exponents. It is not the image of one prescribed operation for every fixed \(k\).',r'\citet[Theorem1.1 and section4, pp.~208--209]{adams1965groupsiii}, received25November1963 and published1965, proves equality of the algebraic quotients. \citet[Theorem1.1]{quillen1971adams} supplies the remaining conjectural relation. \citet[pp.~8--9]{sullivan1974genetics} explains the resulting universality.',[AD3,QUI,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The precise algebraic relation is supplied from Adams. Rank reduction and real versus complex K-theory must be fixed before using the notebook shorthand.')
item(295,'P000492','claim','The purported reverse Adams formula is false for each arbitrary k',r'For every J-trivial \(\xi\), and every \(k\), the notes assert \(\xi=k^N(\psi^k-1)y\), after a uniform \(N\) has been chosen.',r'The assertion is false in the complex K-theory used by the subsequent BU diagrams. \ours\ Let \(x=[L]-1\) generate \(\widetilde{KU}(S^2)\), where \(L\) is the Hopf line bundle. The class \(2x\) has trivial realification and therefore is J-trivial. Since \(x^2=0\), \(\psi^4x=4x\). For \(y=mx\), the asserted equality would require \(2=3\cdot4^N m\), impossible for integers \(m\) and \(N\ge0\). Even allowing negative \(N\) after inverting4 does not remove the factor3.',r'The exact prior theorem is the varying-k subgroup statement of \citet[Theorem1.1 and section4]{adams1965groupsiii}, not the displayed single-image equality. Sullivan\textquotesingle s completed version instead says a J-trivial completed class is \(\eta-\sigma\eta\) for some Galois unit \(\sigma\); see \citet[p.~9, corollary]{sullivan1974genetics}.',[AD3,SUL74],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The counterexample is our verification of the literal quantifiers. The realification vanishes because the clutching loop for \(L\) maps to the nonzero element of \(\pi_1(SO)=\mathbb Z/2\), so twice its class is zero. No uncertain crossed-out word can repair the surviving universal quantifier by itself.',development='annotated',status=r'the displayed universal formula is refuted; Adams\textquotesingle s differently quantified algebraic theorem is established.')
item(295,'P000492','conjecture','The Adams conjecture, already proved before this report',r'The converse is called the Adams conjecture: for every finite complex, some \(N\) makes \(k^N(\psi^k-1)\xi\) J-trivial for all \(k\).',r'For a finite CW-complex \(X\), \(x\in KO(X)\), and an integer \(k\ge2\), there is \(N\ge0\) with \(k^N J(\psi^k x-x)=0\). The analogous complex statement uses underlying real spherical fibrations. A common exponent depending only on \(X\) can be taken: the J-image is finite, and each displayed difference has order supported only on primes dividing \(k\); any \(N\) at least the largest prime-adic valuation of its group exponent kills all such differences.',r'Sullivan\textquotesingle s proof circulated in \citet[Corollary5.9 and pp.~165--166]{sullivan1970geometric}, June1970. The independent proof \citet[Theorem1.1]{quillen1971adams} was received3July1970 and published1971. The expanded Sullivan treatment was published in1974, so it should not be dated as a new post-1973 solution.',[SUL70,QUI],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1972-304',reconstruction='Virtual K-classes, stable J-equivalence and nonnegative exponent are supplied. Uniformity in k follows from the finite J-group and does not repair the false reverse formula.',status='proved; the notebook uses the historical name conjecture for a theorem already available in1970--1971.')
item(295,'P000493','construction','Naturality gives classifying maps for the operations',r'\(\psi^k(f^*\eta)=f^*\psi^k\eta\); thus the operation is specified on the universal bundle over \(BU(n)\). The equation \(\psi^k(1)=1\) permits compatible stabilization.',r'On reduced complex K-theory the natural operation is represented by a map \(BU\to BU\). Its restriction to \(BU(n)\) classifies the virtual class \(\psi^k(\gamma_n)-n\). Compatibility with adding a trivial line follows from additivity and \(\psi^k[1]=[1]\). This yields a stable classifying map, not automatically a lift \(BU(n)\to BU(n)\) for each \(n\).',r'The representing-space construction and the stable versus unstable distinction are explicit in \citet[pp.~161--164, Proposition5.8]{sullivan1970geometric}. This repeats the established operation represented in1971-259.2.',[SUL70],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The rank-zero shift is needed because BU represents reduced K-theory; the unrestricted group is represented by Z times BU.')
item(295,'P000493','claim','Filtered equivalences preserve the intrinsic spherical fibration',r'If an operation \(A:BU\to BU\) preserves dimension, the notes say \(A\xi\sim_J\xi\); they also say that such operations do not occur.',r'The valid sufficient condition is a compatible family of homotopy self-equivalences \(A_n:BU(n)\to BU(n)\), preserving the stabilization filtration. The universal spherical fibration is intrinsic to this filtration, so its stable fibre-homotopy type is fixed. Merely preserving rank, or merely having compatible self-maps, does not suffice: the operation sending a bundle to the trivial bundle of the same rank satisfies both weak descriptions and need not preserve its J-class. Identity and conjugation already refute a literal assertion that no such operations exist.',r'\citet[p.~71, theorem and corollary]{sullivan1974genetics} explicitly requires filtered automorphisms. The earlier \citet[section4, inertia lemma; Corollary5.9]{sullivan1970geometric} uses filtered homotopy equivalences. Proposition5.8 explains the actual obstruction to extending all integral Adams operations unstably.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='Automorphism rather than arbitrary map is the missing hypothesis. The parenthetical word orientations is not adopted as a definition: preservation of filtration and orientation of a sphere bundle are different data.',development='annotated')
item(295,'P000493-P000494','construction','The universal sphere bundle in the BU filtration',r'The inclusion \(BU(n)\to BU(n+1)\) has spheres as fibres; \(U(n)\) acts on \(S^{2n-1}\).',r'The unit-sphere bundle of the universal complex n-plane bundle has total space homotopy equivalent to \(BU(n-1)\), giving \(S^{2n-1}\to BU(n-1)\to BU(n)\). Equivalently the next stabilization has fibre \(S^{2n+1}\). These two indices should not be conflated. The unitary action is the standard action on the unit sphere in \(\mathbb C^n\).',r'\citet[pp.~68--69]{sullivan1974genetics} uses precisely this identification; \citet[p.~166]{sullivan1970geometric} explains the analogous orthogonal filtration and the intrinsic-fibration argument.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The scan leaves the first exponent sign uncertain; mathematical context selects 2n−1 for U(n), while the separate stabilization fibre is correctly 2n+1.')
item(295,'P000493','definition','The stable spherical classifying space and the J-map',r'\(B_F\) is labelled the classifying space of bundles with a homotopy sphere as fibre; J is a functor.',r'Let \(G_m\) be the topological monoid of self-homotopy equivalences of \(S^{m-1}\). Its classifying space parametrizes spherical fibrations up to fibre-homotopy equivalence. Stabilizing by fibrewise join gives \(BG\), and associating a sphere bundle to a vector bundle gives the natural map \(BO\to BG\), or \(BU\to BG\) after realification, representing J. The notebook\textquotesingle s \(B_F\) is identified with this stable classifying space.',r'\citet[section1, p.~69]{quillen1971adams} specifies \(\operatorname{Sph}(X)=\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}_m[X,BG_m]\); \citet[section4]{sullivan1974genetics} develops the localized and completed analogues.',[QUI,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The stable model and monoid, not a Lie group of linear transformations, are specified. A homotopy-sphere fibre means a space with sphere homotopy type.')
item(295,'P000494','construction','Completed spheres and their self-equivalence monoids',r'Sullivan replaces \(S^{2n-1}\) by \(\widehat S^{2n-1}\) and introduces a group \(G_n\), preserving fibrewise homotopy type.',r'Finite or prime completion can be applied fibrewise to spherical fibrations. Fibrations with a completed-sphere fibre are classified by the classifying space of its homotopy self-equivalence monoid. The completed universal fibration over \(\widehat{BU(n)}\) pulls back to the fibrewise completion of the ordinary one. Completed and ordinary fibrations must be distinguished; after stabilization the oriented completed theory recovers the ordinary stable oriented spherical theory.',r'\citet[section4, especially Corollary4, pp.~65--66; p.~69]{sullivan1974genetics} gives this comparison. It was already part of \citet[section4 and Corollary5.9]{sullivan1970geometric}.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The manuscript does not define its G_n. The homotopy self-equivalence monoid is a precise model supporting the displayed classifying-space argument, not a recovered literal definition.',match='partial')
item(295,'P000494','theorem','Sullivan completion and its universal mapping property',r'For simply connected complexes with finitely generated homotopy groups there is a functorial \(X\to\widehat X\), through which maps to spaces with finite homotopy groups factor.',r'For a simply connected CW-complex whose homotopy groups are finitely generated, Sullivan finite completion gives a natural homotopy type \(\widehat X\). For a finite Postnikov target \(F\) with finite homotopy groups, precomposition gives a bijection \([\widehat X,F]\to[X,F]\); the more general formulation uses Sullivan\textquotesingle s compact homotopy functors. Uniqueness is up to homotopy, not uniqueness of a strict map between chosen point-set models.',r'\citet[Theorem3.1 and corollary, p.~44]{sullivan1974genetics} verifies the claim for good homotopy groups, including all groups in the notebook\textquotesingle s simply connected finite-type hypothesis. The construction already appears in \citet[section3]{sullivan1970geometric}.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The homotopy category and the finite Postnikov test objects make the universal claim precise. No naive inverse limit in the category of all ordinary spaces is asserted.')
item(295,'P000494','theorem','Completion of homotopy groups and the product over primes',r'\(\pi_i(\widehat X)=\widehat{\pi_i(X)}\); the groups are profinite and compact, and \(\widehat{\mathbb Z}\) is described through integral p-adic numbers.',r'Under the preceding simply connected finite-generation assumptions, \(\pi_i(\widehat X)\cong\widehat{\pi_i(X)}\) for \(i\ge2\), with its profinite topology. Finite completion of \(\mathbb Z\) is \(\widehat{\mathbb Z}=\varprojlim_m\mathbb Z/m\cong\prod_p\mathbb Z_p\). Completion at one specified prime instead gives \(\mathbb Z_p\).',r'\citet[Theorem3.1 and its proof, pp.~44--46]{sullivan1974genetics} proves the homotopy-group assertion; the prime factors are explained in its section3 and in \citet[section3]{sullivan1970geometric}.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='All primes versus one prime is an explicit distinction. The notebook says described by p-adic integers and does not literally write the incorrect equality Z-hat=Z_p.')
item(295,'P000494','theorem','Finite stable spherical homotopy groups allow extension of J',r'All homotopy groups of the spherical classifying space are finite because they are stable homotopy groups of spheres.',r'For the stable unoriented spherical classifying space \(BG\), \(\pi_1(BG)=\mathbb Z/2\) and \(\pi_i(BG)=\pi_{i-1}^{S}\) for \(i\ge2\); these groups are finite. Its oriented cover \(BSG\) is simply connected with finite homotopy groups. Consequently J extends over finite completion of BU, as used in Sullivan\textquotesingle s proof. The assertion does not include the degree-zero stable stem \(\pi_0^S=\mathbb Z\), nor does it identify the fundamental group with that stem.',r'\citet[p.~67, extension diagram, and Corollary4 on p.~65]{sullivan1974genetics}; the same factorization is displayed in \citet[pp.~164--166]{sullivan1970geometric}.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The shifted positive stems and the exceptional fundamental group are specified; this is why the rank-free stable spherical target is the relevant one.')
item(295,'P000493-P000494','theorem','The Adams operations that become unstable Galois symmetries',r'The operations extend to completed Grassmannians and preserve dimension.',r'For a prime \(p\), an Adams operation \(\psi^k\) with \(p\nmid k\) is the stable shadow of a compatible Galois homotopy symmetry of the p-completed Grassmannians. On full profinite K-theory Sullivan uses the automorphism equal to \(\psi^k\) on p-components with \(p\nmid k\) and to the identity on components with \(p\mid k\). The ordinary \(\psi^k\) for \(|k|>1\) is not itself a profinite automorphism; the omitted prime restriction matters.',r'\citet[Theorem5.7, Proposition5.8 and Corollary5.9, pp.~163--166]{sullivan1970geometric} gives both the symmetry and an obstruction to unrestricted unstable extension. See also \citet[pp.~8--9 and66--69]{sullivan1974genetics}.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The isomorphic part of the operation, rather than the entire completed endomorphism, is supplied. The notebook suppresses precisely this arithmetic restriction.',development='annotated')
item(295,'P000494','construction','Grassmannians over the integers and algebraic Galois action',r'Grassmannians have finitely many algebraic charts over \(\mathbb Z\), so \(\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb C/\mathbb Q)\) acts, discontinuously in the ordinary topology but algebraically in the Zariski and étale settings.',r'The Grassmannian \(\operatorname{Gr}(n,N)\) is a scheme over \(\mathbb Z\). A field automorphism of \(\mathbb C\) fixing \(\mathbb Q\) acts semilinearly on its complex points and on its algebraic covering data. This need not be continuous in the analytic topology, but it induces an automorphism of the geometric étale homotopy type and hence its profinite realization. These actions are compatible with the Grassmannian inclusions.',r'\citet[section5, especially Theorem5.7]{sullivan1970geometric}; the argument from rational defining equations to completed homotopy action is explicit in \citet[pp.~68--69]{sullivan1974genetics}. It repeats the Galois-action theme1971-262.2.',[SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Semilinear action on a scheme and on its covering category replaces a misleading assumption of analytic continuity.')
item(295,'P000494','theorem','Cyclotomic abelianization of the arithmetic action',r'\(\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb C/\mathbb Q)\to\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb Q_{ab}/\mathbb Q)=\widehat{\mathbb Z}^{\times}\).',r'Restricting to \(\overline{\mathbb Q}\) and acting on all roots of unity gives the cyclotomic character into \(\widehat{\mathbb Z}^{\times}\). By the Kronecker--Weber theorem it identifies the abelianization of the absolute Galois group of \(\mathbb Q\). Sullivan proves that its action on stable profinite K-theory factors through this character. Such factorization is not asserted for every finite Grassmannian\textquotesingle s whole unstable homotopy type.',r'\citet[Theorem5.7 and following discussion, pp.~163--175]{sullivan1970geometric} states the abelianization on K-theory and explicitly separates the harder unstable question. The manuscript only needs the stable factorization.',[SUL70],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The absolute algebraic Galois group is distinguished from the much larger automorphism group of C, and stable versus unstable abelianization is kept separate.')
item(295,'P000494','theorem','Analytic and étale comparison has finite coefficients',r'\(H_i(V_c,A)\approx H_i(V_{et},A)\) is written without conditions on A.',r'For a complex algebraic variety of finite type and a finite locally constant coefficient system, analytic and étale cohomology agree under comparison. In the smooth Grassmannian case used here, the profinite analytic homotopy type agrees with the profinite étale homotopy type. With finite constant abelian coefficients the corresponding homology statement follows as well. An integral or rational comparison without completion is not supplied by this theorem.',r'\citet[section12, Comparison theorems]{artinmazur1969etale} is the original reference; this batch checked its publisher metadata. The precise finite-coefficient comparison needed here is read in \citet[p.~68, formula (*) and following paragraph]{sullivan1974genetics} and in the prior \citet[section5]{sullivan1970geometric}.',[AM,SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The notebook uses a homological subscript; the standard cohomological comparison and its finite-coefficient homological consequence are distinguished. Its missing coefficient restriction is essential.')
item(295,'P000494','construction','Nerves recover a profinite homotopy type',r'\(\widehat V_c\sim\varprojlim N(V_{et})\), with N called a nerve.',r'Étale hypercoverings supply a cofiltered system of simplicial homotopy types, the Artin--Mazur étale homotopy type. Completing the system and taking a Sullivan homotopy realization recovers the profinite analytic type in the comparison setting. The displayed inverse limit is shorthand in a pro-homotopy or compact-functor category; taking a point-set inverse limit of arbitrary ordinary covering nerves is not the theorem.',r'\citet[sections8--9 and12]{artinmazur1969etale} names the original construction; its role in Sullivan\textquotesingle s realization is read in \citet[section3, p.~34; p.~68 and footnote]{sullivan1974genetics}. The construction was already available in \citet[section5]{sullivan1970geometric}.',[AM,SUL70,SUL74],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Hypercoverings, profinite completion and the category of the limit are the reconstructed data. This repeats1971-262.1--.3 rather than creating a new open problem.')
correction('P000492','finiteness of the J-quotient',r'K(X)/J-equivalence is finite',r'Use the reduced stable J-group of a finite CW-complex; retaining rank adds Z.',r'Adams1963 section1 explicitly distinguishes the finite reduced group from its unreduced version. The notebook omits reduction.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000492','reverse Adams equality',r'Every J-trivial ξ equals k^N(ψ^k−1)y for every k',r'The topological kernel is described by a subgroup generated using varying k; no arbitrary single k has this property.',r'The scan clearly includes ∀k and a single term. Adams1965 section4 uses a sum over k. The S² class2([L]−1), with k=4, is a direct counterexample.')
correction('P000493','dimension-preserving operation',r'If A preserves dimension then Aξ is J-equivalent to ξ; such A do not occur',r'The sufficient condition is a compatible filtered homotopy automorphism; rank preservation alone fails. Identity and conjugation exist.',r'Sullivan1974 p71 explicitly says filtered automorphism, while the manuscript only draws maps. The trivial-bundle-of-equal-rank operation shows the weaker claim fails.')
correction('P000494','unitary sphere exponent',r'U(n) acts on S^{2n−1}, possibly 2n+1',r'S^{2n−1} for the standard U(n)-action; S^{2n+1} belongs to BU(n)→BU(n+1).',r'Scan review confirms the exponent sign is retraced. The two distinct sphere dimensions occur in the adjacent classifying-space construction.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000493-P000494','unstable completed Adams operations',r'The constructed completed ψ^k extends the Adams operation and preserves dimension',r'Use ψ^k at primes p not dividing k, and its isomorphic part (identity at p dividing k) for full profinite completion.',r'Sullivan1970 Theorem5.7 and Proposition5.8 explicitly make this distinction and show why unrestricted unstable extension fails.')
correction('P000494','analytic/étale comparison',r'H_i(V_c,A)≈H_i(V_et,A)',r'Specify finite coefficient systems and the profinite comparison; the usual primary statement is cohomological.',r'The manuscript supplies no condition on A. Sullivan1974 p68 formula(*) explicitly restricts to finite coefficients.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000494','inverse limit of nerves',r'V_c-hat is the inverse limit of N(V_et)',r'Interpret completed hypercovering nerves as a pro-homotopy type and use a homotopy/compact-functor realization.',r'Sullivan1974 p68, including its footnote, distinguishes completion of nerves and the sense of the limit.',kind='ambiguity')
problem('GP-1972-304','The Adams conjecture for stable spherical fibrations',r'For every finite CW-complex X, integer \(k\ge2\), and virtual real bundle \(x\in KO(X)\), does some \(k^N\) annihilate \(J(\psi^k x-x)\)?',['1972-295.6'],r'proved before the seminar: Sullivan\textquotesingle s June1970 notes, Corollary5.9, and Quillen\textquotesingle s1971 Theorem1.1 (received3July1970). The notebook\textquotesingle s separate reverse formula is false and is not this conjecture.',[SUL70,QUI])
