talk(297,'1973-03-26','A. M. Gabrielov','P000497','Dynkin diagrams of isolated function singularities: opening fragment',r'''Gabrielov\textquotesingle s heading occurs only near the bottom of P000497, after the p-adic material of record298. The surviving fragment introduces the local Milnor fibre and its bouquet of vanishing spheres. No Dynkin graph, intersection-matrix algorithm, sum formula or additional claim is recorded in this assigned fragment. Gabrielov\textquotesingle s1973 paper has the matching opening setup, but its later original theorems cannot be inferred from the title alone.''')
item(297,'P000497','construction','Choosing a Milnor fibre of an isolated critical point',r'For a complex analytic f near0, with an isolated singularity at0, choose a small ball and then a small \(\delta\); consider \(f(x)=\delta\), \(|x|<r_0\).',r'Let \(f:(\mathbb C^n,0)\to(\mathbb C,0)\) be holomorphic with an isolated critical point and \(f(0)=0\). Choose a sufficiently small Milnor radius \(r_0>0\), then \(0<|\delta|\ll r_0\) so the level is smooth and transverse to the bounding sphere. Its intersection with the open ball is a complex \((n-1)\)-manifold, the interior of the compact Milnor fibre.',r'The construction is classical in \citet[sections4--6]{milnor1968singular}. It is repeated in \citet[section1]{gabrielov1973intersection}, received29January1973 and published later in1973. The elementary setup, not Gabrielov\textquotesingle s later matrix theorem, is the exact notebook match.',[MIL,GAB],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The order of choices, nonzero level and isolated critical-point meaning are made explicit. This repeats1972-294.6 and1972-284.11.')
item(297,'P000497','theorem','The bouquet and the reference to vanishing cycles',r'The level is homotopy equivalent to a bouquet of \((n-1)\)-spheres; the final words mention vanishing cycles.',r'The Milnor fibre is homotopy equivalent to a bouquet of \(\mu\) copies of \(S^{n-1}\), where \(\mu=\dim_{\mathbb C}\mathcal O_{\mathbb C^n,0}/(\partial f)\). After a morsification and a distinguished system of paths to its critical values, the vanishing cycles give an integral basis of reduced middle homology. No canonical distinguished basis, Dynkin diagram or intersection signs are fixed by the fragment alone.',r'\citet[Theorems6.5 and7.2]{milnor1968singular} gives the bouquet theorem; \citet[section1]{gabrielov1973intersection} describes the distinguished vanishing-cycle choices. Both results were available before the intervention (the latter manuscript received29January1973).',[MIL,GAB],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The Milnor number and auxiliary choices for a vanishing-cycle basis are supplied. The uncertain sphere-dimension letter is read n, consistently with the complex dimension n−1.',status='the recorded bouquet statement is established; the title alone does not identify an additional research claim.')
correction('P000497','dimension of the vanishing spheres',r'Bouquet of (n−1)-spheres, possibly (k−1)-spheres',r'(n−1)-spheres for a germ on C^n.',r'The scan leaves the first letter retraced, but the preceding manifold dimension is n−1 and Milnor\textquotesingle s theorem gives that sphere dimension.',kind='ambiguity')
save()
print('checkpoint',297,len(items))

