by_id['1972-294.9']['reconstruction']=r'An independent exact matrix check \oursComputed gives the positive E6 Cartan leading principal minors \(2,3,4,5,6,3\), confirming negative definiteness after changing sign; see the accompanying code and output. The identification of the singularity’s lattice is the cited published result.'
by_id['1972-295.5']['statement']=r'''The assertion is false already in integral real K-theory. \ours\ Let \(z\) generate \(\widetilde{KO}(S^4)\cong\mathbb Z\), so \(\psi^kz=k^2z\). Let \(M\ge1\) be the finite order of \(J(z)\), choose a prime \(q\nmid M\), and set \(k=q+1\). Then \(Mz\) is J-trivial, but \(Mz=k^N(\psi^k-1)(mz)\) would require \(M=k^N(k^2-1)m\). The right side is divisible by \(q\) and the left side is not. This holds for every integer \(m\) and every \(N\ge0\), and still holds after inverting \(k\), since \(q\nmid k\). Thus the surviving universal quantifier in k is decisive.'''
by_id['1972-295.5']['reconstruction']=r'''The counterexample is our verification of the literal quantifiers. The sphere-operation formula is checked in Adams1965, p.~207; it also follows by injective complexification and the degree-four Chern character. An independent complex check gives the same verdict: in \(\widetilde{KU}(S^2)=\mathbb Zx\), \(x=[L]-1\), one has \(x^2=0\), \(\psi^4x=4x\), and \(2x\) has trivial realification because \(\widetilde{KO}(S^2)=\mathbb Z/2\). Hence J-triviality of \(2x\) would force \(2=3\cdot4^Nm\), impossible even in \(\mathbb Z[1/4]\). Both arguments were independently checked; neither purports to refute a differently localized or differently quantified statement.'''
by_id['1972-295.5']['refs']=[AD1,AD3,SUL74]
by_id['1972-295.6']['reconstruction']+=r' The uniform-exponent deduction is a finite-group check \ours: if the exponent of J(X) is \(\prod_p p^{a_p}\), take \(N=\max_p a_p\); only p-components with \(p\mid k\) can remain. This was checked prime by prime, including k divisible by more than one prime.'
